{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778869304,"nonce":"FKRlwjwdevuE3wQryraDnuB5Mtw=","message":"Feedbax should have an analogous registry for analyses. Components define executable\nmodel graph nodes; analyses define executable transformations over run manifests,\nevaluation manifests, model checkpoints, trajectory datasets, histories, and derived\ntables/figures.\n\n\nFeedbax already has project-package discovery for analysis modules through\n`ExperimentRegistry`, and `feedbax.analysis.execution` can load analysis modules from\nregistered packages. The analysis framework is already graph-like: `AbstractAnalysis`\ninstances declare typed input ports, input mappings, dependency references, prep/final\nops, optional vmapping/tree mapping, caching, and figure generation. The dependency\nrunner builds and executes a DAG so shared dependencies are not recomputed.\n\nThis is useful and should not be casually replaced, but it is not yet a complete provider\ncontract:\n\n- Analysis modules are discoverable mostly by project/module key.\n- The dependency graph is mostly represented by Python objects and conventions rather\n  than by a portable `AnalysisGraphSpec`.\n- Inputs, outputs, artifact roles, parameters, and compatibility constraints are not\n  consistently machine-readable.\n- Agents cannot reliably ask which analyses are available for a selected run set,\n  checkpoint, trajectory dataset, or evaluation result.\n- Studio and Mandible cannot yet validate an analysis request without knowing\n  project-specific code conventions.\n\n\nIntroduce a logical analysis registry alongside the component registry. A registered\nanalysis should include:\n\n- Stable analysis type ID, such as `feedbax.LinearizationGain` or\n  `rlrmp.SisuFeedbackPerturbation`.\n- Provider/package identity and version.\n- Python import path and callable/builder reference.\n- Human-facing name, description, tags, and display hints.\n- Input schema: accepted manifest kinds, artifact roles, required metadata, and\n  parameter schema.\n- Output schema: emitted manifest kind, artifact roles, table/figure/report products,\n  and metric names.\n- Compatibility predicates, such as required model/task structure, controller type,\n  plant type, run phase, or availability of trajectories.\n- Determinism declaration: whether the analysis is deterministic, stochastic with seed,\n  or narrative/manual.\n- Provenance and review metadata for project-provided or agent-created analyses.\n\nThe registry should let Feedbax answer questions such as:\n\n- Which analyses can run on this training run set?\n- Which analyses require trajectory artifacts rather than only checkpoints?\n- Which analyses emit figures versus numeric tables?\n- Which analyses are deterministic and reproducible?\n- Which analyses are project-specific extensions?\n- Which parameters are required, and can this generated `AnalysisRunSpec` be validated?\n\n\nAnalyses should mirror the component/subgraph distinction:\n\n- **Leaf analysis**: an `AbstractAnalysis` subclass or callable that implements a single\n  compute/figure step. This requires reviewed/importable Python code.\n- **Analysis graph**: a DAG of leaf analyses and data-forwarding nodes, with declared\n  dependency edges, parameters, prep/final ops, and output products. This can be a\n  serialized spec if all referenced leaf analyses resolve through the registry.\n- **Analysis template**: a reusable, parameterized analysis graph offered by Feedbax or a\n  project package, similar to a composite graph template in Studio.\n- **Analysis protocol**: a higher-level procedure that may include evaluation setup,\n  loading models/artifacts, running one or more analysis graphs, and publishing products.\n\nA future `AnalysisGraphSpec` should not discard the current `AbstractAnalysis` design.\nIt should externalize the parts that are currently implicit in Python objects so agents,\nStudio, and orchestrators can validate and reproduce analysis DAGs before running them.\n\nThe likely path is incremental:\n\n1. Keep `AbstractAnalysis` as the leaf implementation model.\n2. Add registry metadata around existing analysis classes/modules.\n3. Define a serializable analysis DAG/spec that references registered analysis IDs.\n4. Let existing project modules compile to or emit that spec.\n5. Only refactor internals where the current object conventions prevent validation,\n   manifests, or reproducibility.\n\nThere is not currently a clear reason to replace the analysis framework wholesale. The\ndeep improvement is to make its graph/dependency structure explicit, validated, and\nmanifested, not to abandon the existing declarative-ish analysis-node pattern.\n\n\nNew projects should be able to propose and register analyses in the same spirit as\nproject components. A project package could expose analyses through an entry point:\n\n```toml\n[project.entry-points.\"feedbax.analyses\"]\nrlrmp = \"rlrmp.feedbax_analyses:register_analyses\"\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```python\ndef register_analyses(registry):\n    registry.register(...)\n```\n\nThe registered callable should be normal reviewed Python code. It may call existing\nFeedbax analysis utilities, project-specific code, or external packages, but its inputs\nand outputs should be declared well enough for validation and manifest emission.\n\n\nAgent-created analyses should follow a review lifecycle similar to leaf components:\n\n1. Draft the analysis code, parameter schema, expected input artifacts, and expected\n   output artifacts.\n2. Validate that it imports and can run on a small fixture or selected run subset.\n3. Declare determinism and seed behavior.\n4. Add focused tests or a reproducible smoke script.\n5. Record provenance: issue, session, commit, author/agent, and review status.\n6. Promote to a stable package/module path before use in durable analysis manifests.\n\nIf a prototype analysis is run before promotion, the resulting `AnalysisRunManifest`\nshould mark that dependency explicitly and include enough provenance to reconstruct or\nreject the result later.\n\n\nReports should be treated as downstream products of analyses, evaluations, or run sets.\nSome report transforms can be registered analyses that emit report artifacts. Narrative\nor agent-authored reports should carry author/provenance metadata and should not be\nmisrepresented as deterministic analysis outputs unless their generation is actually\ndeterministic and reproducible.\n\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}